Q: On Tab press, focused element has a border, but when focusing element manually, it does not have border A: <p>Even though the issue seems to be a bit old, I had recently to struggle with the exact same issue i.e. the focus border shown only on tab press, but not when calling focus(). When the default border is displayed, I also observed it may break the 'functional' aspect of some items like buttons (which border aspect fully disappears at least with opera).</p>
<p>The solution I adopted to ensure consistent focus display is via css (at least it worked for the buttons) e.g. :</p>
<pre><code>*:focus {
  outline: 1px dotted;
  outline-offset : -2px;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Python dictionary keys besides strings and integers? A: <p>Suppose you wanted to execute a list of functions and store the result of each. For each function that raised an exception, you want to record the exception, and you also want to keep a count of how many times each kind of exception is raised.  Functions and exceptions can be used as <code>dict</code> keys, so this is easy:</p>
<pre><code>funclist = [foo, bar, baz, quux]

results    = {}
badfuncs   = {}
errorcount = {}

for f in funclist:
    try:
        results[f] = f()
    except Exception as e:
        badfuncs[f]   = e
        errorcount[type(e)] = errorcount[type(e)] + 1 if type(e) in errorcount else 1
</code></pre>
<p>Now you can do <code>if foo in badfuncs</code> to test whether that function raised an exception (or <code>if foo in results</code> to see if it ran properly), <code>if ValueError in errorcount</code> to see if any function raised <code>ValueError</code>, and so on.</p>

Q: Can I create a dictionary with integer keys using dict()? A: <p>There are also these 'ways':</p>

<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; dict.fromkeys(range(1, 4))
{1: None, 2: None, 3: None}
&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(zip(range(1, 4), range(1, 4)))
{1: 1, 2: 2, 3: 3}
</code></pre>
